10. BREACH, REFUNDS AND TERMINATION

10.1 Use outside the grant must stop immediately; paying for one permitted
     deployment does not authorize other deployments or redistribution. Subject
     to mandatory law, an unremedied material breach may result in termination
     by written notice after 30 days' notice describing the breach and an
     opportunity to cure. Deliberate piracy, key-sharing, unauthorized source
     distribution, fraud or repeated material breach may justify immediate
     written termination. A cure period creates no temporary unlicensed rights.

10.2 A disputed technical result, minor administrative mistake, temporary outage
     or ordinary expiry of updates does not automatically terminate a valid
     perpetual usage grant. A paid grant is not terminable merely for the
     Licensor's convenience. Unrelated compliant grants are not terminated
     solely because another independently identifiable grant is breached.

10.3 Voluntary refund arrangements are those disclosed for the accepted order;
     no additional refund or withdrawal waiver is invented by this document.
     Mandatory remedies and payment-provider requirements prevail. A full
     refund of the license purchase ends that grant, subject to mandatory law;
     a partial adjustment does not unless expressly agreed. A chargeback dispute
     alone is not proof of infringement; actual non-payment or fraud may justify
     appropriate access restrictions and remedies under applicable law.

10.4 After valid termination, cease use under that grant and remove working
     copies and deployments. Legally required inaccessible archives and
     immutable backups may remain until normal expiry if not restored to use.
     Ownership, notices, accrued remedies and the provisions necessary to
     enforce scope survive. No contractual automatic fine or blanket criminal
     liability is imposed; available legal remedies remain applicable.

